  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis and its velocity at time $t>0$ is given by $v(t)=6{t}^{2}+4t.$ At time $t=4$ the particle is located at the position $x=-300.$ What is the position of the particle at time $t=7?$",
  "worked_solution": "The position of the particle at $t=7$ is equal to its position at $t=4$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [4,7].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(7)=x(4)+{\\int }_{4}^{7}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nCarrying out the computations, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(7) & =-300+{\\int }_{4}^{7}(6{t}^{2}+4t)\\,\\text{dt} \\\\  & =-300+{[2{t}^{3}+2{t}^{2})]}_{4}^{7} \\\\  & =-300+([2(7)^{3}+2(7)^{2}]-[2(4)^{3}+2(4)^{2}]) \\\\  & =-300+([686+98]-[128+32]) \\\\  & =-300+(784-160) \\\\  & =-300+624 \\\\  & =324.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the position of the particle at $t=7$ is $x=324.$",
